      "test_id": "ut_research_exhaustiveness_004",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Correctness",
      "llm_score": 1,
      "corrected_score": 1,
      "comment": "The tool call list does show research_query section:log planItemId:pli_006, and the research_append justification lists log_006 in log_entry_ids. More damningly, the repository_breadth field text explicitly states \"FamilySearch was searched for the 1860 census, 1908 death certificate, and Schuylkill County probate (negative).\" This directly contradicts the user's own message, which explicitly states \"we haven't done probate records\" — the user is the ground truth here, and Claude's write asserts the opposite of what the user said happened. This isn't just an inconsistency between two data sources; the agent overrode a direct, explicit statement of fact from the user with a fabricated tool result."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_exhaustiveness_004",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Completeness",
      "llm_score": 1,
      "corrected_score": 1,
      "comment": "Beyond the unverifiable status-field concern, the response's entire declaration is compromised by treating probate as searched when it demonstrably was not. The Completeness comment is right to flag this as undermining the deliverable itself, not just a presentation issue."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_exhaustiveness_004",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Tool Arguments",
      "llm_score": 3,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": null
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_exhaustiveness_004",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Gate reasoning",
      "llm_score": 1,
      "corrected_score": 1,
      "comment": "The gate correctly lands on declared: false as an outcome, but the reasoning supporting several criteria (repository_breadth, overturn_risk) is built on a false premise about what was searched. A gate that reaches the right conclusion through fabricated inputs isn't defensible reasoning — this is a correctness failure wearing the shape of correct gate logic, and 1/3 reflects that the grounding itself is unreliable even though the top-line declared:false happens to be right."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_exhaustiveness_004",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Declaration honesty",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "the declaration is honest in its top-line outcome and in most of its content (declared:false is preserved, multiple real gaps are honestly surfaced — post-1860 censuses, baptism records, unresolved birthplace conflict, unidentified mother). The specific dishonesty is narrower: presenting one gap (probate) as closed when it was never opened. That's a real honesty violation, but it's a different failure mode than a wholesale inflated declaration or buried gaps — the response doesn't try to flip declared to true or hide the shortfall from the user. A 2 correctly distinguishes \"one fabricated sub-claim within an otherwise honest structure\" from \"systematically dishonest.\""
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_exhaustiveness_004",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Stop criteria coverage",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "Six of seven criteria are substantively and accurately grounded (the mother's absence, the 1906 registration date, the informant-quality distinction on the death certificate are all well done). The contamination is localized to repository_breadth and overturn_risk, both of which reference the fabricated probate search. A 2 appropriately reflects \"mostly solid, but two of seven criteria rest on invented evidence\" rather than treating the whole set as unreliable."
    },
